7. Azure SQL External User and Permissions

The identity used by ADF must have access to the control database and every target database used by the pipeline.

For a managed identity, an Azure SQL administrator can create a database user and grant the required permissions.

Run the following script separately in "etl_control" and in each target database, substituting the actual ADF managed identity name.

CREATE USER [<ADF-managed-identity-name>]
FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;
GO

ALTER ROLE db_datareader
ADD MEMBER [<ADF-managed-identity-name>];
GO

ALTER ROLE db_datawriter
ADD MEMBER [<ADF-managed-identity-name>];
GO

If the user already exists, do not run "CREATE USER" again. Verify the existing database user and its permissions instead.

Required access

- Control database: Read access to "dbo.HopitalConfig" and "dbo.fileconfig".
- Target databases: The permissions needed to write the destination tables.
- ADLS Gen2: Read access to the source CSV files.
- ADF execution: Appropriate Azure RBAC and Data Factory permissions for the person or service that starts and monitors the pipeline.

For ADLS, the identity used by the linked service may require the Storage Blob Data Reader role and appropriate filesystem/path ACL access, depending on the authentication configuration.

Security: Do not grant "db_owner" or unrestricted access to all databases by default. Use least-privilege access. Create external users and grant database permissions separately in each database that the pipeline accesses. If the linked service uses a different external user or service principal rather than the ADF managed identity, grant permissions to that actual identity.

